          "derivation": "All four forms are Avestan, and Bartholomae's Altiranisches Wörterbuch col. 1062 has them together: 'j. nāfa- m. \"Nabel\"; g. a) \"Verwandtschaft, Familie\" … Vgl. nāfah- und nabānazdišta-: np. nāf \"Nabel\"'; 'j. nafah- n. \"Verwandtschaft, Familie\"' (Yt. 13.87); 'j. nāfya- Adj. \"familienangehörig\"'. nāfō is the nominative singular of the s-stem (Pokorny quotes 'av. nāfō'). The Iranian -f- where the cognates show -bh- (Skt nābhi-, Av. nabā-nazdišta-) puzzled Horn ('gehen auf indog. bh zurück, während die übrigen Formen auf ph weisen') and Ernout-Meillet ('L'iranien a une forme populaire à *-ph-'); the modern explanation is that -f- was generalized from the nominative *Hnā́fš < *h₃nṓbʰ-s, where *bʰ+s devoiced regularly, and the stem was then thematized as Proto-Iranian *Hnā́fah. The one thing to correct: the label 'Old Persian'. No Old Persian word for 'navel' is attested — Kent's lexicon has no nāfa- (the OP naf-/naθ- of DNb is 'to harm'); the forms are Avestan only, with the Old-Iranian stage reconstructed.",
