          "derivation": "NP ناف nāf 'navel; hub of a wheel; middle, centre' continues Middle Persian nāf (Manichaean nʾp) 'family' / nāfag 'navel' — MacKenzie p.57: 'nāf … family. ~ag … navel' — from Proto-Iranian *Hnā́fah; the Old-Iranian long ā and final -ah were lost in the regular MP apocope (*Hnāfah > nāf), and NP inherits the Pahlavi form unchanged, while nāfag lost its -g in the usual NP way (MP -ag > NP -a/-e), giving نافه. The kinship sense of the Avestan and Pahlavi word survives in NP نواده/نبیره 'grandchild, descendant' (Mo'in's note in Borhan-e Qateʿ lists نواده، نبیر، نبیره beside ناف، نافه), and the 'centre' sense in the idioms ناف زمین/ناف عالم = Mecca. نافه is a straightforward semantic derivative: the musk pod of the Moschus deer sits under the belly skin at the navel — Borhan's footnote spells it out: 'کیسه‌ای مشکین به‌اندازهٔ تخم مرغی که در زیر پوست شکم آهوی خطا قرار دارد و در آن مشک وجود دارد (به همین مناسبت آن را نافه گویند)'. Cognate dialect forms confirm the whole set: Kurdish nāw/nāfik, Ossetic naffä, Baluchi napag/nafag/nafay, Armenian nafat 'musk pod' (borrowed from Persian).",
